## Wildlife-conservation report: aftermath of the 2008 Sichuan earthquake

The 12 May 2008 Wenchuan earthquake struck the Qionglai and Minshan mountain systems, one of the last major strongholds of the giant panda. The immediate concern was not only the safety of pandas in breeding centers, but also the long-term condition and connectivity of wild habitat.

### Panda habitat

The earthquake and its aftershocks triggered extensive landslides, rockfalls and mudslides across panda reserves, including Wolong and neighboring protected areas. These events:

- buried or stripped bamboo forest;
- fragmented habitat and blocked wildlife corridors;
- altered streams and slopes;
- increased the risk of later erosion, flooding and debris flows.

Damage was highly uneven. Entire hillsides were lost in some valleys, while nearby forest remained usable. Early estimates of habitat loss varied because cloud, steep terrain and blocked access made field surveys difficult. Subsequent assessments generally found substantial local destruction rather than the disappearance of the regional panda range as a whole.

Wild pandas were thought to be less vulnerable to collapsing buildings than captive animals, but they faced reduced feeding areas and greater difficulty moving between bamboo patches. Damaged roads and reconstruction activity also threatened to intensify human disturbance in already narrow corridors.

### Captive pandas and damaged roads

The Wolong breeding center, close to the epicenter, suffered severe structural damage. Enclosures and staff facilities collapsed, at least one panda—Mao Mao—was killed, and surviving animals had to be kept in temporary accommodation or transferred to other centers.

Road destruction was a central conservation problem. Landslides cut the main route into Wolong, delaying veterinary access, staff relief, fuel and animal feed. Blocked roads also complicated evacuation and made routine bamboo collection hazardous. Some supplies had to be carried over damaged sections, brought in by emergency convoys when routes reopened, or moved with military and disaster-relief assistance.

### Bamboo deliveries

Adult giant pandas commonly consume roughly 10–20 kilograms of bamboo a day, sometimes more depending on the plant part and season. Because bamboo is bulky, perishable and low in nutrients, maintaining deliveries for a group of captive pandas requires large, frequent shipments.

Emergency priorities included:

1. finding undamaged bamboo stands outside landslide zones;
2. establishing several supply routes rather than relying on one road;
3. moving vulnerable pandas to facilities with dependable local bamboo;
4. monitoring harvested bamboo for dust, sewage, fuel or chemical contamination;
5. maintaining reserves of specialist feed and veterinary supplies.

For wild pandas, importing bamboo into the forest was generally impractical. Protecting intact patches, limiting post-earthquake logging and road expansion, and restoring corridors were more useful than attempting widespread supplementary feeding.

### Possible alternative diets

There is no complete long-term substitute for bamboo. Giant pandas are biologically members of the order Carnivora, but their behavior and digestive ecology are highly specialized around consuming large quantities of bamboo.

In captivity, short-term emergency supplements can include foods already used by experienced panda facilities, such as:

- nutritionally formulated panda biscuits or steamed grain-based cakes;
- apples, carrots and other approved produce;
- bamboo shoots or leaves brought from unaffected regions;
- specialist milk formula for dependent cubs;
- limited protein or vitamin supplements under veterinary supervision.

These foods can supply calories temporarily but cannot safely replace bamboo over an extended period. Sudden dietary changes may cause gastrointestinal problems, and sugary fruit is unsuitable as a staple. Proposals to feed meat simply because pandas are taxonomically carnivores would not provide an appropriate emergency diet without careful veterinary control.

### Conservation outlook

The most important long-term danger was the combination of habitat fragmentation and reconstruction. New roads, quarrying, temporary camps and rebuilding could turn short-lived earthquake damage into permanent loss of panda range. Conservation measures therefore needed to include landslide mapping, habitat restoration with native bamboo, protection of movement corridors, wildlife-sensitive road design, and continued monitoring of isolated panda populations.

The earthquake showed that panda conservation depends on more than breeding centers: resilient transport networks, diversified bamboo supplies, trained emergency teams and connected mountain habitat are all essential to disaster preparedness.
